Subject: DIET/ANTHRO: Lactose intolerance, devel. of dairying, etc.

>>My favorite theory on this is the Montague Alexander one --- that proto-humans
>>went semiaquatic during the Pleistocene drought.  The loss of body hair is one
>>of several important ways in which we resemble sea mammals more than land
>>mammals --- others include the webs between our fingers (monkeys and apes
>>don't have 'em), the subcutaneous-fat layer half of us carry around, noses
>>adapted like a seal's for holding breath while underwater (other primates
>>simply can't do that), the fact that we mate face-to-face, the fact that
>>we communicate using complex sound more than physical motion, the fact that
>>babies can learn to swim before they can learn to walk, and a whole bunch of
>>other things I ain't got time to go into just now.
> 
> Do you really think this theory has any merit? I've never encountered it
> before.

Well, yes.  I do.  I find it an elegant and plausible explanation of lots
of things about human physiology and behavior that would otherwise be
quite mysterious.
 
> Glacial pump?  <squinting suspiciously>

Calvin got on to this by observing that hominids encephalized ridiculously
fast, especially after the Erectus line migrated north out of Africa.  He
thinks the reason was rapid periodic climate changes associated with the
Ice Ages.  Every time the hominid population was just settling into one
adaptive mode, *wham*!  They'd get whipsawed by another glacial advance or
retreat.  According to Calvin, this selected fiercely for brains and
behavioral plasticity, because genetic programs couldn't change fast enough
to cut it.

Unfortunately, having thought about this, I now don't belief that it can be
used to back a claim of more genetic variation specifically in Europeans.  
Climate changes were worldwide, and all modern humans have about the same
degree of encephalization.
