Date: Tue, 15 Jun 93 10:00:47 -0700
From: freeman@MasPar.COM (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: MEDIA:  Jurassic Park

derek writes:

> Sequel ...

I agree, there were lots of sequel hooks.  I think the most interesting
creatures were of course the _Velociraptors_, and there are a number of
other options for dealing them in.  The young in the lab seemed quite
willing to bond with humans; the raptors were clearly breeding in the
wild at the park (at least, I think the tracks around the clutch of wild
eggs were _Velociraptor_ tracks).

It would be particularly interesting to have a sequel that played up the
social aspects of the creatures:  There is one paleontological dig that
provides evidence that _Velociraptor_ was a pack hunter, and at least
among mammals, social carnivores are rather neat to study:  Their
relationships among themselves are usually quite peaceful (which makes
sense in terms of survival -- an animal that fights regularly will sooner
or later get injured (at least) and likely become unable to hunt, and
will die).  The relationships are often rather complicated.  And more
often than not, human beings can get along with them (the mind-blowing
book here is surely Mark and Delia Owens' _Cry_of_the_Kalihari_).  One
could do a quite interesting story based on humans wanting to get along
with _Velociraptor_, or needing to do so for some reason kludged up for
the plot.

Alternatively, one could do something with more fantasy aspects.  Suppose
the _Velociraptors_ of _Jurassic_Park_ were in fact a species with
intelligence on the level of that of humans, who in their original
environment had language, culture, artifacts, et cetera.  As recreated
and raised at the park, they were deprived of a proper raptor upbringing
and ended up rather like the so-called "wild children" who are
supposedly raised without much interaction with other humans.  This line
of speculation could lead to many stories.

Though I must admit that I am fond of the kind of science fiction plot
in which something that at first appears alien and terrifying ends up
sympathetic -- as in the movies _Willard_ and _Ben_, or in _Blade_Runner_.

