Date: Tue, 15 Jun 93 17:58:17 BST
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: color vision

Perry Metzger writes:
>Aliens would not see our paintings or videotapes the way we do --
>similarly, if one is discussing the question of whether some humans
>have different color perception, the question of which colors are
>primary is not meaningless.

I'm not saying the notion is meaningless -- the phenomena of colour mixing
do exist and arise from the nature of human colour receptors.  What I have
difficulty with is the idea that there is a direct sensory experience of
"primariness", as there is of "redness", rather than primariness being
something that must be discovered through experiment.  Green can be made by
mixing yellow and blue; the retinal stimulation that produces the sensation
of green may be regarded as a combination of yellow stimulation and blue
stimulation (*); however, it does not follow that the experience of seeing
green is a mixture of the experience of seeing yellow and the experience of
seeing blue, and the latter assertion is the one I find strange.

(*) I know, Y+B=G is for paint mixing, not light mixing, so someone will
say it's incorrect to apply it to retinal stimulation.  Not so: it depends
how you draw your coordinate frame.  If stimulation is measured by distance
from brightness instead of from darkness, then stimulations will combine by
the paint-mixing rules.  As for the light-mixing rule that B+G=Y, I
remember being quite incredulous on discovering this rule as a child.  I
simply could not imagine (and still can't, even though a close look at a TV
shows it happening before my eyes) how blue and green could possibly give
yellow.  But maybe some other people can.

