Date: Wed, 16 Jun 1993 03:48 EST
From: X91007@pitvax.xx.rmit.edu.au
Subject: color vision

The three types of cone - red, green and blue - or L, M and 
S appear to encode three channels of information:

1. L-M  or M-L:  whether there is more red or green in a 
certain part of the visual field.

2. (L+M)-S or (-L-M)+S: which encodes whether there is more 
yellow or blue.

3. (L+M+S) or (-L-M-S): which encodes the luminance.

This is why three colours are need to represent the colour 
space and why these colours are red, green and blue. The 
reason yellow is also percieved as a pure colour is a 
direct resuly of #2. 

A simple test this channel hypothesis is to fatigue one of 
the channels by using it constantly for a while. For 
instance stare at the sun and you will see dark spots; 
stare at green and you will see a red afterimage. More 
subtly it explains why a violet (blue plus red) or a pure 
red image will both produce green afterimages.

As I understand it goldfish are actually tertachromatic, 
that is they need four colours to represent the colour 
space; the fourth cone detects in the ultra-violet. 
Persumably if we were tetrachromatic we would also 
experience other colours as pure.

A good introduction into the psychophysics of colour vision 
is: Mollon, J.D. (1982) Colour vision. %Annual reviews of 
psychology%, *33*, 41-85.

