    On the subject of marriage and longevity, studies of general
health, esp. stress related problems, have shown that relationships
with _women_ are strongly correlated to better general health. (Just
what the radical feminists have been telling us, "woman good, man
bad".) 
    When a heterosexual couple marry, the man's general health and
life expectancy go up, but the woman's stays the same. When a couple
move to a different city, the woman's health is more likely to suffer
than the man's.
    The most likely theory I've heard to explain this is that women
form a network of friendships with other women, but often the only
"real" friend a man has (in terms of the kind of relationships woman
usually form) is his wife/girlfriend or a woman friend. The deep,
feeling oriented interactions typical of these relationships seem to
be very healthy.


    To switch over to the "why aren't there more women on this list?"
thread, I share an office with two women. (Both programmers, one says
she always wanted to be a computer nerd, and dressed in imitation of
one she particularly admired when she was a teen.) When they talk, and
when their women friends come over to talk, they don't talk about
technical or scientific stuff (unless it is directly work related).
They talk about people, feelings, who did what, and all of the other
"gossip" men look down on.

    A semi-related note on men-women differences, if you have ever
been mystified by the other sex (no matter which sex you are) read Dr.
Tannen's (I hope I spelled that right) book _You_ _Just_ _Don't_
_Understand_. She is a linguist, and the book is about the differences
between femalespeak and malespeak. (She finds consistant
cross-cultural patterns.) Just about every other page would remind me
of a conversation I had with a woman (usually a former girlfriend) and
I would think, "So _that's_ what was going on!"

